From 159608bfc986a6dca38fbc5971da922071a464c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 08:51:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Enforce import attributes instead of parsing and dropping
 them
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Import attributes parsed everywhere the grammar allows and nothing acted on
them: nothing read AstImportStatement.Attributes, the three `export … from`
forms discarded theirs outright, and the compiler's call to the loader passed
only the specifier. So `with { type: 'json' }` — the portable form, and the
only one a browser accepts on a JSON module — was accepted and ignored, and so
was `with { flavour: 'nonsense' }`. Silence is the one answer an assertion
mechanism must never give.

Where each failure is raised is measured from Chromium rather than chosen, and
the split turns out to be principled. On a static declaration the keys are
literals, so a key outside the vocabulary and a duplicate key are early
SyntaxErrors, decided in the parser. Whether the `type` VALUE names a module
type, and whether the module it resolves to is of that type, depends on the
module, so both are load-time TypeErrors raised by the host. A dynamic
import()'s keys are a runtime value and can only be judged at load, so it
reports both as TypeErrors — which is exactly what Chromium does. Every message
is Chromium's own.

Attributes reach the host in one of two shapes and are validated through one
path. A dynamic import passes its runtime options object in slot 1, with the
full object-shape validation the spec asks for (options an object, `with` an
object, every value a string). A static declaration has no options object, so
its clause travels in slot 2 as a flat array of alternating key/value strings —
flat because every part of it is a literal the grammar already fixed, and in
source order so the FIRST offending key is the one reported. `export … from`
now carries its clause through AstExportStatement to the same place, so all
three forms are enforced like the import they perform.

The type/module match is the one rule this host cannot implement the way the
web does. A browser checks the assertion against the response MIME type; there
are no MIME types here, so it checks the resolved module key — the same fact by
the only means available, and the one that already decided the module would be
parsed as JSON, so the check and the parse cannot disagree.

`css` is told apart from a typo: it is a real module type and a real capability
this engine does not have, so it reports that rather than "not a valid module
type". A page can distinguish "not implemented here" from "not a thing".

One deliberate divergence, pinned by a test: a .json module imported with NO
attribute still loads, where a browser rejects it. There the attribute defends
against a server returning JSON where script was expected — a mismatch that
cannot arise in a host whose locally resolved key is itself the type. This
context also serves require, which has no attributes at all, so demanding one
from import would make the two halves of one host disagree about one file.

Thirty-three regressions in ImportAttributeEnforcementTests, twenty-three of
which fail before this change; each error-name assertion goes through a dynamic
import so the JS-visible name, not just the message, is pinned.
ModuleAttributeClauseTests keeps every grammar case it pinned but retargeted:
it was written against `with { type: 'javascript' }`, which is not a module type
any platform defines and which enforcement now correctly rejects, so each case
now uses the one clause valid on a module this host serves. It also gains the
empty clause, which is the only one a JavaScript module can carry.

Engine suites: modules 160/160, module-extensions 5/5, built-ins 2215/2215,
integration 5167/5168, compiler 1400/1402, parser 198/198, core/runtime/ast
clean — the three failures pre-existing and unrelated.
